@prefix : <http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix sosi: <http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@base <http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#> .

<http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi/3.1.0> ;
    owl:priorVersion <http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi/3.0.0> ;
    owl:versionInfo "3.1.0" ;
    dcterms:title "SOSI: SOcial SItuation ontology for robotics"@en ;
    dcterms:description """SOSI is a formal vocabulary for describing social situations observed by robots in human environments. A social situation (sosi:SocialSituation) is decomposed into interpretable, compositional constructs organised along Burke's dramatistic pentad: Act (sosi:InteractionState — what is happening, i.e. affect, communicative behaviour and spatial configuration), Scene (sosi:SocioPhysicalContext — the socio-physical setting), Agent (sosi:AgentsConstruct — participant structure), Agency (sosi:InteractionStructure — the interactional mechanism), and Purpose (sosi:InteractionPurpose — whether the interaction is goal-directed).

Each dimension is modelled as a class taxonomy whose leaf values are also provided as canonical named individuals (value-partition pattern), so that scene annotations can link a SocialSituation directly to a value (e.g. :scene42 sosi:hasCardinality sosi:dyad). Dimension roots are annotated with sosi:isClosedDimension and sosi:isSingleValued, which state whether the value set is closed under a covering axiom and whether the corresponding property is functional. Closed dimensions additionally carry disjointness and AllDifferent axioms, and cross-dimension coherence is enforced by the situation patterns in the "Situation patterns" section, so that a DL reasoner detects contradictory annotations. Incomplete annotations are detected by classification against sosi:FullyAnnotatedSituation rather than by inconsistency, since under open-world semantics a missing value is not a contradiction.

SOSI is a labelling vocabulary. It is deliberately decoupled from any particular dataset, generation pipeline or model: it carries no prompt templates, no release-specific dimension profiles and no dataset provenance. It describes the externally observable interactional configuration of a scene, not the participants' internal mental states."""@en ;
    dcterms:created "2025-01-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:source "Burke, K. (1969). A Grammar of Motives. University of California Press." ;
    rdfs:label "SOSI: Robotic Social Situation Ontology"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Concrete perception-level states referenced by this ontology (e.g. engagement) may be grounded against the Open Robot Ontology (ORO, http://kb.openrobots.org#) in deployed systems."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Changes in 3.0.0 relative to 2.0.0:
 (1) The Act branch no longer conflates value partitions, presence flags and cue inventories: sosi:GroupProxemics and sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement moved under the new grouping sosi:SpatialConfiguration, and sosi:NonVerbalCommunication was narrowed to sosi:NonverbalCue (genuine cues only), so that sosi:hasNonverbalCue can no longer be pointed at a dimension value or a presence flag.
 (2) sosi:Speaking became the closed dimension sosi:SpeechActivity with an explicit sosi:SpeechAbsent value, so speech absence is asserted rather than inferred from a missing triple; sosi:hasSpeechActivity is now functional.
 (3) sosi:SocialRelationType gained sosi:MixedRelation, a covering axiom, and a functional property, matching the mixed-value pattern of the other participant dimensions.
 (4) Cross-dimension coherence axioms were added (see "Situation patterns").
 (5) Terms were renamed for clarity; every 2.0.0 IRI survives as an owl:deprecated alias (see "Deprecated 2.0.0 aliases")."""@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Changes in 3.1.0 relative to 3.0.0:
 (1) Four participant/scene dimensions were added: sosi:Gender (male/female), sosi:AgeRange (young/mid-age/old) and sosi:Ethnicity (six values) under sosi:AgentsConstruct, and sosi:Activity (open, extensible) under sosi:InteractionState. Each is attributed collectively to the central interacting unit, not to individual agents. A mixed-gender value is deliberately not modelled, so a mixed-gender group is left unannotated on sosi:Gender.
 (2) The sosi:LargeGroup/sosi:Crowd distinction (previously separated only by sosi:participantsIndividuallyCountable) was removed: the two were merged into sosi:LargeGroup (11+ participants); sosi:Crowd is now an owl:deprecated alias.
 (3) The crowd-density dimension was renamed sosi:SocialBackground -> sosi:CrowdDensity (property sosi:hasSocialBackground -> sosi:hasCrowdDensity); the former names survive as owl:deprecated aliases.
 (4) The definition of sosi:SituationFormality was clarified: formality is conveyed by both the physical/social context (scene) and participants' clothing, and where they conflict the context dominates (an office with casual clothing is still formal). This is an annotation guideline, not an enforced OWL axiom."""@en .


#################################################################
#    Annotation properties
#################################################################

skos:prefLabel rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:altLabel rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .

sosi:isClosedDimension rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "is closed dimension"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "True when the dimension root carries a covering axiom, i.e. its value set is complete and no further values may be added by a deployment. False for intentionally extensible vocabularies (sosi:NonverbalCue, sosi:Location)."@en .

sosi:isSingleValued rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "is single valued"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "True when the property annotating this dimension is functional, i.e. a situation carries exactly one value. Dimensions whose values may legitimately co-occur are marked false."@en .

sosi:minParticipants rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "minimum participants"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lower bound on the number of interaction participants covered by a sosi:Cardinality value. Machine-readable replacement for the numeric ranges that were previously stated only in prose."@en .

sosi:maxParticipants rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "maximum participants"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Upper bound on the number of interaction participants covered by a sosi:Cardinality value. Omitted where the value is open-ended."@en .

sosi:participantsIndividuallyCountable rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "participants individually countable"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the participants of a cardinality value can be individually counted (true for solo/dyad/triad/small group). In 2.0.0–3.0.0 this flag was the sole boundary between sosi:LargeGroup and sosi:Crowd; those two values were merged in 3.1.0, so the flag is now purely descriptive and no longer separates any two values."@en .


#################################################################
#    Object Properties
#################################################################

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasCardinality
sosi:hasCardinality rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:Cardinality ;
    rdfs:label "has cardinality"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to its group-size category (solo, dyad, triad, small group, large group). Exactly one value per situation. Counts the interaction participants only; surrounding bystanders are described by sosi:hasCrowdDensity instead."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasCrowdDensity
sosi:hasCrowdDensity rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:CrowdDensity ;
    rdfs:label "has crowd density"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the density of surrounding background people (crowded / not crowded). Describes the context around the interaction, not the interacting group itself, which is covered by sosi:hasCardinality. Named sosi:hasCrowdContextBg in 2.x and sosi:hasSocialBackground in 3.0.0; renamed to sosi:hasCrowdDensity in 3.1.0."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasEmotionalValence
sosi:hasEmotionalValence rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:AffectiveValence ;
    rdfs:label "has emotional valence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the perceived affective tone of the situation (negative, neutral, positive), as manifested through observable cues such as posture, facial expressions and interaction dynamics — not the internal emotional state of individual agents."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasEngagementLevel
sosi:hasEngagementLevel rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:EngagementLevel ;
    rdfs:label "has engagement level"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the participants' engagement state (engaged, disengaged, mixed). 'Mixed' is the single value used when participants differ in engagement, so the property is functional. Scored over the interaction participants counted by sosi:hasCardinality only, never over the background people described by sosi:hasCrowdDensity; consequently a solo situation cannot be mixed (see sosi:SoloSituation)."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasFormality
sosi:hasFormality rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:SituationFormality ;
    rdfs:label "has formality"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the formality of its social setting (formal / informal). Formality is conveyed jointly by the physical/social context (the scene) and by the participants' clothing/dress. Annotation guideline for the reasoner: where the two conflict, the context dominates — e.g. a person in an office wearing a t-shirt is still formal. This precedence is a definitional guideline, not an enforced OWL axiom (clothing and context are not separately modelled)."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasInteractionType
sosi:hasInteractionType rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:InteractionType ;
    rdfs:label "has interaction type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the mechanism of interaction (cooperative, competitive, persuasive, conforming, observational). Deliberately not functional: distinct mechanisms may co-occur in one episode. The pairwise disjointness of the value classes is intentional and does not conflict with multi-valued use — it states that no single value is simultaneously two mechanisms, not that a situation exhibits only one."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasInterdependenceLevel
sosi:hasInterdependenceLevel rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:InterdependenceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "has interdependence level"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the degree of coordination required between participants (high / low interdependence)."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasLocation
sosi:hasLocation rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:Location ;
    rdfs:label "has location"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the place type where it occurs (e.g. office, kitchen, street). Location individuals are deployment-specific and intentionally not enumerated in this ontology."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasNonverbalCue
sosi:hasNonverbalCue rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:NonverbalCue ;
    rdfs:label "has nonverbal cue"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the nonverbal cues observed in it (eye contact, body language, gestures, vocalizations). Not functional: several cues typically co-occur. The range is restricted to genuine cues; interpersonal distance and body orientation are dimensions in their own right, annotated with sosi:hasProxemicsValue and sosi:hasRelativeBodyPlacement, and the present/absent summary is sosi:hasNonverbalSignaling. In 2.0.0 the range admitted all three, which allowed nonsensical assertions such as sosi:hasNonverbalCue sosi:nonverbalAbsent."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasNonverbalSignaling
sosi:hasNonverbalSignaling rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:NonverbalSignaling ;
    rdfs:label "has nonverbal signaling"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to whether the interaction relies on observable nonverbal behaviour (present / absent). A summary flag over sosi:hasNonverbalCue: the two are kept coherent by sosi:NonverbalAbsentSituation and sosi:CuedSituation, so that a situation can never be annotated as signal-free while also carrying cues."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasEnvironmentType
sosi:hasEnvironmentType rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:EnvironmentType ;
    rdfs:label "has environment type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the indoor/outdoor character of its physical environment. Renamed from sosi:hasPhysicalType in 3.0.0 to match its range."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasProxemicsValue
sosi:hasProxemicsValue rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:GroupProxemics ;
    rdfs:label "has proxemics value"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the group-level interpersonal distance zone (intimate, personal, social, public, or mixed when participants occupy different zones). Not applicable to solo situations, which have no interpersonal distance."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasRelationType
sosi:hasRelationType rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:SocialRelationType ;
    rdfs:label "has social relation type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the structural relationship holding between participants (peer–peer, leader–follower, or mixed when different participant pairs differ). Functional as of 3.0.0: heterogeneous cases take the single value sosi:mixedRelation, matching the pattern already used for engagement, proxemics and body placement. Not applicable to solo situations."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasRelativeBodyPlacement
sosi:hasRelativeBodyPlacement rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement ;
    rdfs:label "has relative body placement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the dominant relative body orientation of participants (facing, side by side, back to back, or mixed). Not applicable to solo situations, which have no relative orientation."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasSpeechActivity
sosi:hasSpeechActivity rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:SpeechActivity ;
    rdfs:label "has speech activity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to its verbal activity: either sosi:speechAbsent, or one of the speech types (giving speech, informal chatting, shouting, whispering). Functional as of 3.0.0. Speech absence is asserted explicitly rather than represented by the absence of a triple, which under open-world semantics could not be distinguished from 'not yet annotated'. The defined class sosi:SpeechPresent groups the four speech types, so the property serves both as a presence indicator and as a type."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasInteractionPurpose
sosi:hasInteractionPurpose rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:InteractionPurpose ;
    rdfs:label "has interaction purpose"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to its purpose category: goal-directed (participants pursue a shared objective) or aimless (socially driven exchange without a defined task goal)."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasParticipantGender
sosi:hasParticipantGender rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:Gender ;
    rdfs:label "has participant gender"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the gender of its central interacting unit, attributed collectively to the participants (never to the background people). Added in 3.1.0. A mixed-gender value is intentionally not modelled, so a group whose participants differ in gender is left unannotated on this dimension."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasAgeRange
sosi:hasAgeRange rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:AgeRange ;
    rdfs:label "has age range"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the age range of its central interacting unit, attributed collectively to the participants. Added in 3.1.0."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasEthnicity
sosi:hasEthnicity rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:Ethnicity ;
    rdfs:label "has ethnicity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the ethnicity attributed to its central interacting unit. Added in 3.1.0. A coarse, dataset-defined enumeration; it captures an externally apparent grouping for annotation, not a claim about the participants' identity."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasActivity
sosi:hasActivity rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range sosi:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "has activity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a SocialSituation to the coarse current activity of its central interacting unit (e.g. handling a phone, a routine task, observing, moving through the space). Added in 3.1.0. Open/extensible: deployments may add further activity values, so the dimension carries no covering axiom."@en .


#################################################################
#    Data properties
#################################################################

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#hasPurposeDescription
sosi:hasPurposeDescription rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosi:SocialSituation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "has purpose description"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Free-text description of the task, purpose or goal of the social interaction. Typically used together with sosi:hasInteractionPurpose sosi:goalDirected. Renamed from sosi:hasPurpose in 3.0.0 to remove the collision with sosi:hasInteractionPurpose."@en .


#################################################################
#    Classes
#################################################################

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SocialSituation
sosi:SocialSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    owl:disjointWith sosi:SocialSituationConstruct ;
    rdfs:label "Social situation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An interaction episode to be annotated (a meeting, a request, a collaboration, ...). A SocialSituation is described compositionally by linking it, through the sosi:has* object properties, to values of the pentad construct dimensions."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SocialSituationConstruct
sosi:SocialSituationConstruct rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Social situation construct"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Root of the descriptive dimensions used to characterise a SocialSituation, organised along Burke's dramatistic pentad: Act (InteractionState), Scene (SocioPhysicalContext), Agent (AgentsConstruct), Agency (InteractionStructure) and Purpose (InteractionPurpose)."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Burke, K. (1969). A Grammar of Motives. University of California Press." .


### ============================================================
###  ACT — InteractionState
### ============================================================

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#InteractionState
sosi:InteractionState rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialSituationConstruct ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction state (Act)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "What is happening in the situation: the observable interactional state, expressed through affective tone, communicative behaviour, the spatial configuration of participants and the central unit's current activity."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso "Burke's dramatistic pentad: the 'act' element."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:AffectiveValence sosi:CommunicationModality sosi:SpatialConfiguration sosi:Activity ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#Activity
sosi:Activity rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionState ;
    rdfs:label "Activity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The coarse current activity of the central interacting unit — what the participant(s) are doing. Open and extensible: deployments may introduce further activities, so this dimension carries no covering axiom. Single-valued: sosi:hasActivity is functional. The values below are the initial vocabulary."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension false ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:HandlingPhone rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Handling a phone"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "handling a phone in hand"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The central unit is handling a phone in hand (looking at, holding or using a mobile device)."@en .

sosi:RoutineTask rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Routine task"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "engaging in a routine task"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The central unit is engaging in an ordinary, non-noteworthy task."@en .

sosi:ObservingSurroundings rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Observing the surroundings"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "observing the surroundings"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The central unit is looking around / observing the surrounding environment."@en .

sosi:MovingThroughSpace rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Moving through the space"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "moving through the space"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The central unit is walking or otherwise moving through the space."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:HandlingPhone sosi:RoutineTask sosi:ObservingSurroundings sosi:MovingThroughSpace ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#AffectiveValence
sosi:AffectiveValence rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionState ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:NegativeValence sosi:NeutralValence sosi:PositiveValence ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Affective valence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Affective polarity (pleasantness) expressed or perceived in the situation, used to tag whether the episode feels positive, neutral or negative. Refers to the situation as a whole, not to individual agents' internal states."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:NegativeValence rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AffectiveValence ;
    rdfs:label "Negative"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "negative valence"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "negative atmosphere"@en , "unpleasant"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Overall negative/unpleasant affect (frustration, tension, discomfort). Examples: raised-voice argument; visible annoyance while being interrupted."@en .

sosi:NeutralValence rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AffectiveValence ;
    rdfs:label "Neutral"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "neutral valence"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "neutral atmosphere"@en , "emotionally flat"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Neither clearly positive nor negative affect; emotionally flat or task-focused. Examples: routine check-in with neutral tone; quiet waiting in a queue."@en .

sosi:PositiveValence rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AffectiveValence ;
    rdfs:label "Positive"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "positive valence"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "positive atmosphere"@en , "pleasant"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Overall positive/pleasant affect in the situation (warm, friendly, satisfied). Examples: smiling greetings; enthusiastic praise after success."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:NegativeValence sosi:NeutralValence sosi:PositiveValence ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#CommunicationModality
sosi:CommunicationModality rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionState ;
    rdfs:label "Communication modality"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Grouping (not itself a dimension) for the communicative aspects of the situation: the verbal activity dimension, the nonverbal presence flag, and the nonverbal cue inventory."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:SpeechActivity sosi:NonverbalSignaling sosi:NonverbalCue ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SpeechActivity
sosi:SpeechActivity rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:CommunicationModality ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:SpeechAbsent sosi:SpeechPresent ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Speech activity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The verbal activity observed in the situation: absent, or one of the four speech types. Renamed from sosi:Speaking in 3.0.0 and closed with an explicit absence value."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:SpeechAbsent rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpeechActivity ;
    owl:disjointWith sosi:SpeechPresent ;
    rdfs:label "Speech absent"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "no speech"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "silent"@en , "speech absent"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "No verbal communication is taking place. Examples: two people working silently side by side; a person waiting alone without speaking."@en .

sosi:SpeechPresent rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpeechActivity ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:GivingSpeech sosi:InformalChatting sosi:Shouting sosi:Whispering ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Speech present"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "At least one person in the social situation is using verbal communication. A defined class over the four speech types, so that sosi:hasSpeechActivity answers both 'is anyone speaking?' and 'what kind of speech?' without a second property."@en .

sosi:GivingSpeech rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpeechPresent ;
    rdfs:label "Giving speech"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "giving a speech"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "presenting"@en , "lecturing"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "One participant addresses others in a sustained, largely one-directional way. Examples: a lecture; a toast; a formal presentation. Compatible with sosi:solo (a person rehearsing a speech alone)."@en .

sosi:InformalChatting rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpeechPresent ;
    rdfs:label "Informal chatting"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "informal chatting"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "small talk"@en , "casual conversation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Conversational, multi-directional talk at ordinary volume with relaxed turn-taking. Examples: small talk at a coffee machine; friends chatting."@en .

sosi:Shouting rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpeechPresent ;
    rdfs:label "Shouting"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "shouting"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "raised voice"@en , "calling out"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Speech at markedly raised volume, whether hostile (argument) or functional (calling someone at a distance, noisy environment)."@en .

sosi:Whispering rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpeechPresent ;
    rdfs:label "Whispering"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "whispering"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "hushed speech"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Speech at markedly reduced volume, typically to restrict the audience or comply with quietness norms (library, ceremony)."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:SpeechAbsent sosi:GivingSpeech sosi:InformalChatting sosi:Shouting sosi:Whispering ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#NonverbalSignaling
sosi:NonverbalSignaling rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:CommunicationModality ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:NonverbalPresent sosi:NonverbalAbsent ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Nonverbal signaling"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the interaction relies on observable nonverbal behaviour at all (present / absent). A summary flag over sosi:NonverbalCue, which lists the specific cues; in 2.0.0 this was misplaced as a kind of nonverbal communication rather than a statement about it."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:NonverbalPresent rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalSignaling ;
    rdfs:label "Present"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "nonverbal signalling present"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Observable nonverbal signalling is present in the situation."@en .

sosi:NonverbalAbsent rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalSignaling ;
    rdfs:label "Absent"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "nonverbal signalling absent"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "No observable nonverbal signalling in the situation. A situation annotated this way must carry no sosi:hasNonverbalCue assertion (see sosi:NonverbalAbsentSituation)."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:NonverbalPresent sosi:NonverbalAbsent ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#NonverbalCue
sosi:NonverbalCue rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:CommunicationModality ;
    rdfs:label "Nonverbal cue"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A family of observable nonverbal communicative acts. Renamed from sosi:NonVerbalCommunication in 3.0.0 and narrowed to genuine cues: interpersonal distance and body orientation moved to sosi:SpatialConfiguration, and the present/absent flag to sosi:NonverbalSignaling. Deliberately left open (no covering axiom) so deployments may add cue families."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension false ;
    sosi:isSingleValued false .

sosi:EyeContact rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalCue ;
    rdfs:label "Eye contact"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "eye contact"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "mutual gaze"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "At least one person has established eye contact with another; it may or may not be reciprocal."@en .

sosi:BodyLanguage rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalCue ;
    rdfs:label "Body language"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "body language"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "posture"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Communicative posture and body movement other than discrete gestures (e.g. leaning in, crossed arms, nodding)."@en .

sosi:Gesture rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalCue ;
    rdfs:label "Gesture"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "gesture"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Discrete communicative hand/arm movement. Subclasses follow the standard deictic / iconic / symbolic (emblem) distinction. Not a leaf value: annotate one of the subclasses."@en ;
    dcterms:source "McNeill, D. (1992). Hand and Mind: What Gestures Reveal about Thought. University of Chicago Press." .

sosi:DeicticGesture rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Gesture ;
    rdfs:label "Deictic gesture"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "deictic gesture"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "pointing"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Pointing gesture that indicates an object, person, direction or location in the environment."@en .

sosi:IconicGesture rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Gesture ;
    rdfs:label "Iconic gesture"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "iconic gesture"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Gesture whose form depicts a concrete aspect of what is being communicated (e.g. mimicking the shape or size of an object)."@en .

sosi:SymbolicGesture rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Gesture ;
    rdfs:label "Symbolic gesture"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "symbolic gesture"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "emblem"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Conventionalised gesture (emblem) with a culturally agreed meaning independent of speech (e.g. thumbs-up, wave, OK sign)."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:DeicticGesture sosi:IconicGesture sosi:SymbolicGesture ) .

sosi:NonverbalVocalization rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalCue ;
    rdfs:label "Nonverbal vocalization"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "nonverbal vocalization"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Audible, non-linguistic vocal expression (laughter, crying, sighing, etc.). Not a leaf value: annotate one of the subclasses."@en .

sosi:Laughter rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalVocalization ;
    rdfs:label "Laughter"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "laughter"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "At least one person is laughing."@en .

sosi:Crying rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:NonverbalVocalization ;
    rdfs:label "Crying"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "crying"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "At least one person is crying."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Laughter sosi:Crying ) .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:EyeContact sosi:BodyLanguage sosi:Gesture sosi:NonverbalVocalization ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SpatialConfiguration
sosi:SpatialConfiguration rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionState ;
    rdfs:label "Spatial configuration"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Grouping (not itself a dimension) for the spatial organisation of participants: interpersonal distance and relative body orientation. Introduced in 3.0.0 so that these value partitions are no longer nested under the nonverbal cue inventory. Retained under the Act element, following the pentad assignment used in the SOSI paper."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kendon, A. (1990). Conducting Interaction: Patterns of Behavior in Focused Encounters. Cambridge University Press. (F-formation system)" .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:GroupProxemics sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#GroupProxemics
sosi:GroupProxemics rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpatialConfiguration ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:IntimateProxemics sosi:PersonalProxemics sosi:SocialProxemics sosi:PublicProxemics sosi:MixedProxemics ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Group proxemics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Group-level assessment of interpersonal distance, using Hall's proxemic zones. 'Mixed' means different people in the group are positioned within different proxemic zones."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hall, E. T. (1966). The Hidden Dimension. Doubleday." ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:IntimateProxemics rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupProxemics ;
    rdfs:label "Intimate"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "intimate zone"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants within Hall's intimate zone (roughly < 0.45 m): embracing, whispering distance."@en .

sosi:PersonalProxemics rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupProxemics ;
    rdfs:label "Personal"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "personal zone"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants within Hall's personal zone (roughly 0.45–1.2 m): interactions among friends or well-acquainted people."@en .

sosi:SocialProxemics rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupProxemics ;
    rdfs:label "Social"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "social zone"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants within Hall's social zone (roughly 1.2–3.7 m): interactions among acquaintances or in professional settings."@en .

sosi:PublicProxemics rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupProxemics ;
    rdfs:label "Public"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "public zone"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants within Hall's public zone (roughly > 3.7 m): public speaking or distant co-presence."@en .

sosi:MixedProxemics rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupProxemics ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "mixed proxemic zones"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Different people in the group are positioned within different proxemic zones."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:IntimateProxemics sosi:PersonalProxemics sosi:SocialProxemics sosi:PublicProxemics sosi:MixedProxemics ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#RelativeBodyPlacement
sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SpatialConfiguration ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Facing sosi:SideBySide sosi:BackToBack sosi:MixedPlacement ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Relative body placement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "How participants' bodies are oriented relative to one another (facing toward/away; alignment), an observable proxy for the shared interactional space of the group."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kendon, A. (1990). Conducting Interaction: Patterns of Behavior in Focused Encounters. Cambridge University Press. (F-formation system)" ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:Facing rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement ;
    rdfs:label "Facing"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "facing"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "vis-à-vis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants' bodies are oriented toward one another (vis-à-vis arrangement)."@en .

sosi:SideBySide rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement ;
    rdfs:label "Side by side"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "side by side"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants are aligned next to each other, oriented in the same direction."@en .

sosi:BackToBack rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement ;
    rdfs:label "Back to back"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "back to back"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants' bodies are oriented away from one another."@en .

sosi:MixedPlacement rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:RelativeBodyPlacement ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "mixed placement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "No single dominant arrangement: different participants show different relative orientations."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Facing sosi:SideBySide sosi:BackToBack sosi:MixedPlacement ) .


### ============================================================
###  SCENE — SocioPhysicalContext
### ============================================================

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SocioPhysicalContext
sosi:SocioPhysicalContext rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialSituationConstruct ;
    rdfs:label "Socio-physical context (Scene)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The socio-physical setting that shapes norms and interaction expectations: place type, physical environment, social background and situational formality."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso "Burke's dramatistic pentad: the 'scene' element."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:PhysicalEnvironment sosi:SocialEnvironment ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#PhysicalEnvironment
sosi:PhysicalEnvironment rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocioPhysicalContext ;
    rdfs:label "Physical environment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Grouping (not itself a dimension) for properties of the physical setting that shape interaction norms: the indoor/outdoor character and the place type."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:EnvironmentType sosi:Location ) .

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#EnvironmentType
sosi:EnvironmentType rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:PhysicalEnvironment ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:IndoorEnvironment sosi:OutdoorEnvironment ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Environment type (indoor/outdoor)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Indoor/outdoor character of the physical environment."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:IndoorEnvironment rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:EnvironmentType ;
    rdfs:label "Indoor"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "indoor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The situation takes place inside a built, enclosed space."@en .

sosi:OutdoorEnvironment rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:EnvironmentType ;
    rdfs:label "Outdoor"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "outdoor"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The situation takes place in an open-air, unenclosed space."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:IndoorEnvironment sosi:OutdoorEnvironment ) .

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#Location
sosi:Location rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:PhysicalEnvironment ;
    rdfs:label "Location (place type)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The place type where the situation occurs (office, kitchen, street, shop, ...). Deliberately open: deployments instantiate their own Location individuals rather than choosing from a closed list."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension false ;
    sosi:isSingleValued false .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SocialEnvironment
sosi:SocialEnvironment rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocioPhysicalContext ;
    rdfs:label "Social environment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Grouping (not itself a dimension) for the social character of the setting where the situation occurs: its normative formality and the surrounding social background."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:SituationFormality sosi:CrowdDensity ) .

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SituationFormality
sosi:SituationFormality rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialEnvironment ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:FormalSituation sosi:InformalSituation ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Situation formality"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "How strict the setting's social norms and expectations are (etiquette, role clarity, professionalism). Formality is conveyed jointly by the physical/social context (the scene) and by the participants' clothing/dress. Annotation guideline for the reasoner: where the two conflict, the context dominates — e.g. a person in an office wearing a t-shirt is still formal, and someone in a suit at a home kitchen is still informal. This precedence is a definitional guideline, not an enforced OWL axiom (clothing and context are not separately modelled). Examples: job interview (formal); friends hanging out (informal)."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:FormalSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SituationFormality ;
    rdfs:label "Formal"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "formal"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Formal setting, conveyed by the context (scene) together with dress; the context takes precedence when the two disagree, so a formal scene (e.g. an office) is formal even if a participant is dressed casually. Examples: courtroom hearing; corporate presentation room; an office meeting where someone wears a t-shirt."@en .

sosi:InformalSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SituationFormality ;
    rdfs:label "Informal"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "informal"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "casual"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Informal/casual setting with relaxed norms and a more personal tone, conveyed by the context together with dress; the context takes precedence, so a casual scene stays informal even if a participant is formally dressed. Examples: kitchen conversation at home; chatting at a café with friends."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:FormalSituation sosi:InformalSituation ) .

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#CrowdDensity
sosi:CrowdDensity rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialEnvironment ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Crowded sosi:NotCrowded ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Crowd density"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Density of surrounding background people — a contextual condition that shapes the situation without being part of the interaction mechanics. Counts bystanders only; the interacting group is covered by sosi:Cardinality. Named sosi:Background_Participants in 1.x, sosi:CrowdDensity in 2.0.0, renamed sosi:SocialBackground in 3.0.0, and restored to sosi:CrowdDensity in 3.1.0."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:Crowded rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:CrowdDensity ;
    rdfs:label "Crowded"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "crowded background"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "High density of surrounding people; the interaction is embedded in a crowded context."@en .

sosi:NotCrowded rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:CrowdDensity ;
    rdfs:label "Not crowded"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "uncrowded background"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Few or no surrounding background people."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Crowded sosi:NotCrowded ) .


### ============================================================
###  AGENT — AgentsConstruct
### ============================================================

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#AgentsConstruct
sosi:AgentsConstruct rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialSituationConstruct ;
    rdfs:label "Agents construct (Agent)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Characteristics of the social agents taking part in the situation: how many they are, how they relate, how engaged they are, and the gender, age range and ethnicity attributed to the central unit collectively."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso "Burke's dramatistic pentad: the 'agent' element."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Cardinality sosi:SocialRelationType sosi:EngagementLevel sosi:Gender sosi:AgeRange sosi:Ethnicity ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#Cardinality
sosi:Cardinality rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgentsConstruct ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Solo sosi:GroupCardinality ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Cardinality (group size)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Group-size category — how many interaction participants are involved in the episode. Even minimal changes in cardinality (dyad vs triad) produce qualitatively different participation structures. Bystanders are not counted here; see sosi:CrowdDensity."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Egbert, M. (2012). Conversation Analysis and Dyads, Triads, and More. Research on Language and Social Interaction 45(4); Gonzalez, R. & Griffin, D. (2004). Measuring Individuals in a Social Environment. SAGE Handbook of Methods in Social Psychology." ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:Solo rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Cardinality ;
    owl:disjointWith sosi:GroupCardinality ;
    rdfs:label "Solo"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "solo"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "single person"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Exactly 1 participant: a single-person situation (others absent or not relevant). Examples: a person practicing a speech alone; someone waiting alone at a kiosk."@en ;
    sosi:minParticipants 1 ;
    sosi:maxParticipants 1 ;
    sosi:participantsIndividuallyCountable true .

sosi:GroupCardinality rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Cardinality ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Dyad sosi:Triad sosi:SmallGroup sosi:LargeGroup ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Group"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "group"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Two or more interaction participants. A defined class over the four multi-person values, so that the coarse solo/group contrast used in the first SOSI dataset release is expressible without introducing a second set of labels. Not a leaf value: annotate one of the subclasses."@en ;
    sosi:minParticipants 2 .

sosi:Dyad rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupCardinality ;
    rdfs:label "Dyad"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "dyad"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "pair"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Exactly 2 participants interacting one-to-one. Examples: customer and cashier exchanging a request; two colleagues discussing a document."@en ;
    sosi:minParticipants 2 ;
    sosi:maxParticipants 2 ;
    sosi:participantsIndividuallyCountable true .

sosi:Triad rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupCardinality ;
    rdfs:label "Triad"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "triad"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Exactly 3 participants interacting. Examples: three students discussing homework; a manager with two team members in a brief huddle."@en ;
    sosi:minParticipants 3 ;
    sosi:maxParticipants 3 ;
    sosi:participantsIndividuallyCountable true .

sosi:SmallGroup rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupCardinality ;
    rdfs:label "Small group"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "small group"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "4 to 10 participants engaged in a shared episode. Examples: 5–8 people in a project stand-up; a small family discussing plans."@en ;
    sosi:minParticipants 4 ;
    sosi:maxParticipants 10 ;
    sosi:participantsIndividuallyCountable true .

sosi:LargeGroup rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:GroupCardinality ;
    rdfs:label "Large group / crowd"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "large group or crowd"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "crowd"@en , "large group"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "More than 10 participants. Examples: a classroom; an all-hands meeting; a station concourse at rush hour; a concert audience. As of 3.1.0 this value covers both individually-countable large groups and uncountable crowds: the earlier sosi:Crowd value (which split this range by countability rather than by a participant threshold) was merged in, since the countability boundary is hard to perceive reliably."@en ;
    sosi:minParticipants 11 .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Solo sosi:Dyad sosi:Triad sosi:SmallGroup sosi:LargeGroup ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SocialRelationType
sosi:SocialRelationType rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgentsConstruct ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:PeerRelation sosi:LeaderFollower sosi:MixedRelation ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Social relation type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Structural relationship between participants (status/authority symmetry vs asymmetry). Examples: two peers collaborating; a leader delegating to a follower. Closed and single-valued as of 3.0.0: heterogeneous groups take sosi:mixedRelation."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sawyer, R. (2019). Dialogic Status in Design Education: Authority and Peer Relations in Studio Class Conversations. Social Psychology Quarterly 82." ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:PeerRelation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialRelationType ;
    rdfs:label "Peer–peer"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "peer relation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Symmetric relation — participants have similar status/authority and interact as equals. Examples: classmates working together; two coworkers at the same level coordinating."@en .

sosi:LeaderFollower rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialRelationType ;
    rdfs:label "Leader–follower"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "leader-follower relation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Asymmetric relation — one participant leads/directs and the other follows/assists. Examples: supervisor giving instructions to an intern; tour guide leading a group member."@en .

sosi:MixedRelation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialRelationType ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "mixed relations"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Different relation types hold between different participant pairs — e.g. a supervisor with two peers who are equals to each other. Added in 3.0.0 so that heterogeneous groups have a value, matching the mixed-value pattern of engagement, proxemics and body placement."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:PeerRelation sosi:LeaderFollower sosi:MixedRelation ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#EngagementLevel
sosi:EngagementLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgentsConstruct ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Engaged sosi:Disengaged sosi:MixedEngagement ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Engagement level"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "How engaged participants are in the interaction (attention, involvement, responsiveness). Examples: mutual attention and turn-taking (engaged); one person distracted by a phone (disengaged). Scored over the interaction participants only. Concrete perception-level states may be grounded against ORO individuals (e.g. oro:engaged) in deployed systems."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:Engaged rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:EngagementLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Engaged"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "engaged"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants are attentive, involved and responsive to one another."@en .

sosi:Disengaged rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:EngagementLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Disengaged"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "disengaged"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants are co-present but not attentive or responsive to one another (e.g. absorbed in individual tasks)."@en .

sosi:MixedEngagement rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:EngagementLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "mixed engagement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants differ in engagement: some are engaged while others are disengaged. Requires at least two participants, so it is incompatible with sosi:solo (see sosi:SoloSituation)."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Engaged sosi:Disengaged sosi:MixedEngagement ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#Gender
sosi:Gender rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgentsConstruct ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Male sosi:Female ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Gender"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Gender attributed collectively to the central interacting unit (not to the background people, and not asserted per individual agent). Added in 3.1.0. A mixed-gender value is intentionally not modelled: a group whose participants differ in gender is left unannotated on this dimension."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:Male rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Gender ;
    rdfs:label "Male"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "male"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The central interacting unit is male (a single man, or a group of men)."@en .

sosi:Female rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Gender ;
    rdfs:label "Female"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "female"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The central interacting unit is female (a single woman, or a group of women)."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Male sosi:Female ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#AgeRange
sosi:AgeRange rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgentsConstruct ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Young sosi:MidAge sosi:Old ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Age range"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Age band attributed collectively to the central interacting unit. Added in 3.1.0."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:Young rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgeRange ;
    rdfs:label "Young"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "young"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Young age band (roughly children, adolescents and young adults)."@en .

sosi:MidAge rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgeRange ;
    rdfs:label "Mid-age"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "middle-aged"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Middle age band (roughly adults in mid-life)."@en .

sosi:Old rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgeRange ;
    rdfs:label "Old"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "old"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "elderly"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Older age band (roughly older adults / the elderly)."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:Young sosi:MidAge sosi:Old ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#Ethnicity
sosi:Ethnicity rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:AgentsConstruct ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:White sosi:Black sosi:EastAsian sosi:SouthAsian sosi:Latino sosi:MiddleEastern ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Ethnicity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Ethnicity attributed collectively to the central interacting unit. Added in 3.1.0. A coarse, dataset-defined enumeration capturing an externally apparent grouping for annotation, not a claim about the participants' identity."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:White rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Ethnicity ;
    rdfs:label "White"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "white"@en .

sosi:Black rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Ethnicity ;
    rdfs:label "Black"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "black"@en .

sosi:EastAsian rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Ethnicity ;
    rdfs:label "East Asian"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "east asian"@en .

sosi:SouthAsian rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Ethnicity ;
    rdfs:label "South Asian"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "south asian"@en .

sosi:Latino rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Ethnicity ;
    rdfs:label "Latino"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "latino"@en .

sosi:MiddleEastern rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:Ethnicity ;
    rdfs:label "Middle Eastern"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "middle eastern"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:White sosi:Black sosi:EastAsian sosi:SouthAsian sosi:Latino sosi:MiddleEastern ) .


### ============================================================
###  AGENCY — InteractionStructure
### ============================================================

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#InteractionStructure
sosi:InteractionStructure rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialSituationConstruct ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction structure (Agency)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The means by which interaction is achieved, formalised as an interactional mechanism and organisation rather than a physical tool: the mechanism of interaction (cooperative, competitive, ...) and the degree of coordination required between participants."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso "Burke's dramatistic pentad: the 'agency' element."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:InteractionType sosi:InterdependenceLevel ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#InteractionType
sosi:InteractionType rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionStructure ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:CooperativeInteraction sosi:CompetitiveInteraction sosi:PersuasiveInteraction sosi:ConformingInteraction sosi:ObservationalInteraction ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The mechanism of interaction between participants. Covers both goal/outcome-organised mechanisms (cooperative, competitive, persuasive — formerly grouped as SocialDynamics) and norm-organised mechanisms (conforming, observational — formerly grouped as SocialNormsFollowing). The only closed dimension that is multi-valued: mechanisms may co-occur within one episode."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued false .

sosi:CooperativeInteraction rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionType ;
    rdfs:label "Cooperative"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "cooperative"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants pursue aligned goals or mutual benefit; coordination is supportive. Examples: two people jointly assembling furniture; teammates sharing information to solve a problem."@en .

sosi:CompetitiveInteraction rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionType ;
    rdfs:label "Competitive"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "competitive"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants pursue opposed or scarce outcomes (win/lose trade-off). Examples: rivals competing for the same prize; bidders competing in an auction."@en .

sosi:PersuasiveInteraction rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionType ;
    rdfs:label "Persuasive"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "persuasive"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "One participant aims to change another's beliefs, decisions or actions. Examples: a salesperson pitching a product; a friend convincing someone to join an event."@en .

sosi:ConformingInteraction rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionType ;
    rdfs:label "Conforming"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "conforming"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Behaviour primarily reflects compliance with expected norms/rules. Examples: greeting with customary politeness in a formal setting; following safety instructions during a drill."@en .

sosi:ObservationalInteraction rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionType ;
    rdfs:label "Observational"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "observational"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Predominantly observing/monitoring rather than directly coordinating with others. Examples: a security guard watching a crowd; a passerby observing a street performance."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:CooperativeInteraction sosi:CompetitiveInteraction sosi:PersuasiveInteraction sosi:ConformingInteraction sosi:ObservationalInteraction ) .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#InterdependenceLevel
sosi:InterdependenceLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionStructure ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:HighInterdependence sosi:LowInterdependence ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Interdependence level"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "How strongly participants' outcomes or choices depend on one another; whether sustaining joint activity requires coordination among participants. Examples: tightly coordinated handover (high); parallel independent work in the same room (low)."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johnson, D. W. & Johnson, R. T. (1989). Cooperation and Competition: Theory and Research. Interaction Book Company. (social interdependence theory)" ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:HighInterdependence rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InterdependenceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "High interdependence"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "high interdependence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Strong mutual dependence — coordination is required and outcomes are linked. Examples: carrying a table together; synchronising steps during a joint procedure."@en .

sosi:LowInterdependence rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InterdependenceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Low interdependence"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "low interdependence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Weak dependence — participants' outcomes are mostly separable. Examples: two people working on separate laptops at the same table; individuals browsing independently in a shop."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:HighInterdependence sosi:LowInterdependence ) .


### ============================================================
###  PURPOSE — InteractionPurpose
### ============================================================

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#InteractionPurpose
sosi:InteractionPurpose rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:SocialSituationConstruct ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:GoalDirectedInteraction sosi:AimlessInteraction ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction purpose (Purpose)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the situation is oriented toward a shared task goal or unfolds without an explicit objective. Separates interactions in which participants coordinate to achieve a specific outcome from interactions that occur for social exchange without a defined task goal."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso "Burke's dramatistic pentad: the 'purpose' element."@en ;
    sosi:isClosedDimension true ;
    sosi:isSingleValued true .

sosi:GoalDirectedInteraction rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionPurpose ;
    rdfs:label "Goal-directed"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "goal-directed"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Participants pursue a shared, identifiable objective (a task, decision or outcome). Examples: collaborative work; planning who does what in a project. The free-text goal can be recorded with sosi:hasPurposeDescription."@en .

sosi:AimlessInteraction rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosi:InteractionPurpose ;
    rdfs:label "Aimless"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "aimless"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "socially driven"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The interaction has no predefined or clearly identifiable goal; it is socially driven. Example: informal chat between colleagues about their weekend."@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:GoalDirectedInteraction sosi:AimlessInteraction ) .


### ============================================================
###  Pentad families are pairwise disjoint
### ============================================================

[] rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( sosi:InteractionState sosi:SocioPhysicalContext sosi:AgentsConstruct sosi:InteractionStructure sosi:InteractionPurpose ) .


#################################################################
#    Situation patterns — cross-dimension coherence
#################################################################
#
#  Defined subclasses of sosi:SocialSituation that carry the coherence axioms.
#  They are named rather than anonymous general concept inclusions so that a
#  reasoner reports which pattern an annotation violated, and so that tooling
#  that does not handle GCIs still applies them.
#
#  Deliberately NOT constrained, so that the omissions read as decisions:
#    * sosi:largeGroup + sosi:notCrowded — a large interacting group in an otherwise
#      empty space is unusual but possible; the background ring is independent
#      of the group.
#    * sosi:solo + sosi:givingSpeech — the sosi:Solo comment's own example is a
#      person rehearsing a speech alone.
#    * sosi:aimless + sosi:cooperative — chatting can be cooperative without a
#      task goal.

###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SoloSituation
sosi:SoloSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:intersectionOf ( sosi:SocialSituation
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                 owl:onProperty sosi:hasCardinality ;
                                                 owl:someValuesFrom sosi:Solo ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                      owl:onProperty sosi:hasRelationType ;
                      owl:maxCardinality "0"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                      owl:onProperty sosi:hasProxemicsValue ;
                      owl:maxCardinality "0"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                      owl:onProperty sosi:hasRelativeBodyPlacement ;
                      owl:maxCardinality "0"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                      owl:onProperty sosi:hasEngagementLevel ;
                      owl:allValuesFrom [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Engaged sosi:Disengaged ) ] ] ;
    rdfs:label "Solo situation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A situation with a single interaction participant. It has no interpersonal relation, no interpersonal distance and no relative body orientation, so the corresponding properties must be left unasserted rather than given a value; and since engagement is scored over the participants only, it cannot be mixed."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#NonverbalAbsentSituation
sosi:NonverbalAbsentSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:intersectionOf ( sosi:SocialSituation
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                 owl:onProperty sosi:hasNonverbalSignaling ;
                                                 owl:someValuesFrom sosi:NonverbalAbsent ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                      owl:onProperty sosi:hasNonverbalCue ;
                      owl:maxCardinality "0"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] ;
    rdfs:label "Nonverbal-absent situation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A situation annotated as having no observable nonverbal signalling. It must therefore carry no nonverbal cue. In 2.0.0 the flag and the cue list could contradict each other freely."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#CuedSituation
sosi:CuedSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:intersectionOf ( sosi:SocialSituation
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                 owl:onProperty sosi:hasNonverbalCue ;
                                                 owl:someValuesFrom sosi:NonverbalCue ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                      owl:onProperty sosi:hasNonverbalSignaling ;
                      owl:allValuesFrom sosi:NonverbalPresent ] ;
    rdfs:label "Cued situation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A situation in which at least one nonverbal cue was observed. Any nonverbal signalling flag it carries can only be 'present' — the converse direction of sosi:NonverbalAbsentSituation. Note that this constrains an asserted flag rather than supplying a missing one: under open-world semantics a cued situation with no flag asserted stays silent about it, and remains consistent."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#SignalFreeSituation
sosi:SignalFreeSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:intersectionOf ( sosi:SocialSituation
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                 owl:onProperty sosi:hasSpeechActivity ;
                                                 owl:someValuesFrom sosi:SpeechAbsent ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                 owl:onProperty sosi:hasNonverbalSignaling ;
                                                 owl:someValuesFrom sosi:NonverbalAbsent ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                      owl:onProperty sosi:hasEngagementLevel ;
                      owl:allValuesFrom [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                          owl:unionOf ( sosi:Disengaged sosi:MixedEngagement ) ] ] ;
    rdfs:label "Signal-free situation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Neither speech nor nonverbal signalling is observable — the 'weakly communicative co-presence' case. Participants cannot be jointly engaged, because engagement is itself only observable through speech or nonverbal cues such as mutual gaze. This is the one interpretive axiom in this section rather than a structural necessity; drop it if a deployment grounds engagement in a modality SOSI does not model."@en .


###  http://www.phd-project.org/ontology/sosi#FullyAnnotatedSituation
sosi:FullyAnnotatedSituation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:intersectionOf ( sosi:SocialSituation
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasCardinality ;          owl:someValuesFrom sosi:Cardinality ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasEmotionalValence ;     owl:someValuesFrom sosi:AffectiveValence ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasFormality ;            owl:someValuesFrom sosi:SituationFormality ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasEngagementLevel ;      owl:someValuesFrom sosi:EngagementLevel ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasSpeechActivity ;       owl:someValuesFrom sosi:SpeechActivity ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasNonverbalSignaling ;   owl:someValuesFrom sosi:NonverbalSignaling ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasEnvironmentType ;      owl:someValuesFrom sosi:EnvironmentType ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasCrowdDensity ;        owl:someValuesFrom sosi:CrowdDensity ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasInterdependenceLevel ; owl:someValuesFrom sosi:InterdependenceLevel ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasInteractionPurpose ;   owl:someValuesFrom sosi:InteractionPurpose ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasAgeRange ;             owl:someValuesFrom sosi:AgeRange ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasEthnicity ;           owl:someValuesFrom sosi:Ethnicity ]
                                               [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosi:hasActivity ;            owl:someValuesFrom sosi:Activity ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Fully annotated situation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A situation carrying a value for every single-valued dimension that applies to any situation regardless of cardinality. Incompleteness is detected by classification against this class rather than by inconsistency: under open-world semantics a missing value is not a contradiction, so a subclass axiom would be inert. The interpersonal dimensions (relation type, proxemics, relative body placement) are excluded because they do not apply to sosi:SoloSituation, as is the multi-valued sosi:hasInteractionType and the open sosi:hasLocation. sosi:hasParticipantGender is also excluded: because a mixed-gender group has no value, requiring it would make such scenes permanently incomplete. Age range, ethnicity and activity are required (added in 3.1.0)."@en .


#################################################################
#    Individuals (canonical values)
#################################################################
#
#  One canonical named individual per leaf value class. Non-leaf classes
#  (sosi:Gesture, sosi:NonverbalVocalization, sosi:SpeechPresent,
#  sosi:GroupCardinality) intentionally have none: annotate a leaf.

### Cardinality
sosi:solo rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Solo ; rdfs:label "Solo"@en .
sosi:dyad rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Dyad ; rdfs:label "Dyad"@en .
sosi:triad rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Triad ; rdfs:label "Triad"@en .
sosi:smallGroup rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:SmallGroup ; rdfs:label "Small group"@en .
sosi:largeGroup rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:LargeGroup ; rdfs:label "Large group / crowd"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:solo sosi:dyad sosi:triad sosi:smallGroup sosi:largeGroup ) .

### Affective valence
sosi:negative rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:NegativeValence ; rdfs:label "Negative"@en .
sosi:neutral rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:NeutralValence ; rdfs:label "Neutral"@en .
sosi:positive rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:PositiveValence ; rdfs:label "Positive"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:negative sosi:neutral sosi:positive ) .

### Activity
sosi:handlingPhone rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:HandlingPhone ; rdfs:label "Handling a phone"@en .
sosi:routineTask rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:RoutineTask ; rdfs:label "Routine task"@en .
sosi:observingSurroundings rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:ObservingSurroundings ; rdfs:label "Observing the surroundings"@en .
sosi:movingThroughSpace rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:MovingThroughSpace ; rdfs:label "Moving through the space"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:handlingPhone sosi:routineTask sosi:observingSurroundings sosi:movingThroughSpace ) .

### Engagement level
sosi:engaged rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Engaged ; rdfs:label "Engaged"@en .
sosi:disengaged rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Disengaged ; rdfs:label "Disengaged"@en .
sosi:mixedEngagement rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:MixedEngagement ; rdfs:label "Mixed"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:engaged sosi:disengaged sosi:mixedEngagement ) .

### Gender
sosi:male rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Male ; rdfs:label "Male"@en .
sosi:female rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Female ; rdfs:label "Female"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:male sosi:female ) .

### Age range
sosi:young rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Young ; rdfs:label "Young"@en .
sosi:midAge rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:MidAge ; rdfs:label "Mid-age"@en .
sosi:old rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Old ; rdfs:label "Old"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:young sosi:midAge sosi:old ) .

### Ethnicity
sosi:white rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:White ; rdfs:label "White"@en .
sosi:black rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Black ; rdfs:label "Black"@en .
sosi:eastAsian rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:EastAsian ; rdfs:label "East Asian"@en .
sosi:southAsian rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:SouthAsian ; rdfs:label "South Asian"@en .
sosi:latino rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Latino ; rdfs:label "Latino"@en .
sosi:middleEastern rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:MiddleEastern ; rdfs:label "Middle Eastern"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:white sosi:black sosi:eastAsian sosi:southAsian sosi:latino sosi:middleEastern ) .

### Situation formality
sosi:formal rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:FormalSituation ; rdfs:label "Formal"@en .
sosi:informal rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:InformalSituation ; rdfs:label "Informal"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:formal sosi:informal ) .

### Crowd density
sosi:crowded rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:Crowded ; rdfs:label "Crowded"@en .
sosi:notCrowded rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual , sosi:NotCrowded ; rdfs:label "Not crowded"@en .

[] rdf:type owl:AllDifferent ;
   owl:distinctMembers ( sosi:crowded sosi:notCrowded ) .

